# Authentication
Source: https://openrouter.ai/docs/api_reference/authentication

API Authentication

You can cover model costs with OpenRouter API keys.

Our API authenticates requests using Bearer tokens. This allows you to use `curl` or the [OpenAI SDK](https://platform.openai.com/docs/frameworks) directly with OpenRouter.

> [!WARNING]
>
> API keys on OpenRouter are more powerful than keys used directly for model APIs.
>
> They allow users to set credit limits for apps, and they can be used in [OAuth](/docs/guides/overview/auth/oauth) flows.

## Accepted credentials

Send one credential per request in the `Authorization` header as `Bearer <credential>`. For clients built on the Anthropic SDK, you can also send an API key (not an access token) in the `x-api-key` header. OpenRouter accepts the following credential types:

| Credential | Format | How you get it |
| - | - | - |
| API key | `sk-or-v1-` followed by 64 lowercase hexadecimal characters, for example `sk-or-v1-0123…cdef` (73 characters in total). | Create one on the [API keys page](https://openrouter.ai/keys), or receive one through [OAuth PKCE](/docs/guides/overview/auth/oauth). [Management keys](/docs/guides/overview/auth/management-api-keys) use the same format. |
| OpenRouter access token | A signed JWT (three base64url segments separated by `.`) with `typ: at+jwt`, issuer `https://openrouter.ai`, and an `inference` or `management` scope. Signed with `ES256`; public keys are at `https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/oauth/jwks`. Valid for at most 15 minutes. | Exchange a JWT from your own identity provider with [workload identity federation](/docs/guides/overview/auth/workload-identity-federation). |
| OpenRouter sign-in (OIDC) access token | A signed JWT issued by `https://clerk.openrouter.ai` that carries the `inference` scope. | Sign in to OpenRouter through an OIDC client, for example [Claude Desktop with OpenRouter sign-in](/docs/cookbook/coding-agents/claude-desktop-oidc). |

Send the credential exactly as issued, with no extra whitespace or quotes. OpenRouter treats a bearer that starts with `sk-or-v1-` as an API key. It treats a JWT as an access token only when its issuer is `https://openrouter.ai` or `https://clerk.openrouter.ai`, and rejects any other bearer. OpenRouter also rejects an expired JWT and a JWT without the scope the endpoint requires. Some endpoints accept only an API key, so an access token with the `inference` scope doesn't replace an API key everywhere. To use a token from your own identity provider, exchange it for an OpenRouter access token first; OpenRouter doesn't accept third-party JWTs directly.

## Troubleshooting 401 errors

A rejected credential returns `401 Unauthorized`. The error message tells you what OpenRouter received:

| Message | Cause | Fix |
| - | - | - |
| `Invalid API key: this is not an OpenRouter API key` | The `Authorization` or `x-api-key` value is a key from another provider: it starts with `sk-` but not `sk-or-`. OpenAI and Anthropic SDKs fall back to `OPENAI_API_KEY` or `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` when you don't pass a key explicitly. | Pass your OpenRouter key explicitly, for example `apiKey: process.env.OPENROUTER_API_KEY`, and check that `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` is set in the process that makes the request. |
| `No API key in the Authorization header` | The header is `Bearer` followed by nothing, `undefined`, or `null`. This usually means the environment variable holding your key is unset. | Set the variable, then restart the shell or app that reads it. |
| `Missing Authentication header` | The `Authorization` header is malformed, for example it has extra spaces, or its value isn't a recognized credential. | Send exactly `Authorization: Bearer <OPENROUTER_API_KEY>`, with one space and no quotes. |
| `Malformed authentication credential` | The key contains characters that can't be sent in an HTTP header, often invisible characters picked up during copy-paste. | Copy the key again from the [API keys page](https://openrouter.ai/keys). |
| `API key expired` | The key passed its expiration date. | Create a new key. |
| `Invalid API key` (with nothing after it) or `User not found.` | The key has the OpenRouter format but was deleted, disabled, or never existed. | Check the key on the [API keys page](https://openrouter.ai/keys), or create a new one. |

A request with no credentials at all (no `Authorization` header, no `x-api-key` header, and no session cookie) returns `No cookie auth credentials found` on endpoints that also accept a signed-in session, such as `/api/v1/chat/completions`. Other endpoints may return `Missing Authentication header` instead.

## Using an API key

To use an API key, [first create your key](https://openrouter.ai/keys). Give it a name and choose a credit limit (or no limit). Once a key has used its credit limit, requests with it are rejected until you raise the limit on the key's page, or, for a daily, weekly, or monthly limit, until the limit resets. Keys created for you during onboarding get a \$100 credit limit and expire after 180 days.

> [!WARNING]
>
